From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ep9
Subject: [PATCH] Add Regex::exec_into: searches with caller-owned memory

Each search allocated its backtrack stack and its loop and group
vectors (MatchAttempter::new), then the captures vector of the Match
and a clone of the group names. A caller that runs many searches (a
JavaScript engine runs one per exec, many per replace or split) paid
five allocations per search.

exec_into runs one search (or, with anchored, one attempt at start)
over a Text with Limits. The engine's stacks come from a Scratch that
the caller keeps across searches, and the capture ranges go into the
caller's Vec, so a search allocates nothing once both have grown. No
Match is built, so the group names are not cloned.

For this the backtrack stack entries are keyed by the position type,
and the UTF-16, UCS-2 and Latin-1 inputs use IndexPosition<'static>
(a plain index), so one Scratch serves every text and regex. The
executor's search loops now return a span; next_match builds the Match.

Test: tests/scratch.rs (one Scratch for 6 regexes, 6 texts, 3 text
forms, search and anchored, equal to find_limited and
match_at_limited; a stopped search leaves a usable Scratch).
